WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.610
Film Courage: How often are you looking for
scripts to purchase?

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.200
Jay Silverman, producer/director/photographer:
Constantly.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.722
Film Courage: What is the process?

00:00:05.722 --> 00:00:06.722
Where are you looking?

00:00:06.722 --> 00:00:12.390
Jay: I’m looking right now for a script
for my next movie and working on ideas at

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.040
the same time.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.710
We’ve had some bumps in the road.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:27.830
I tried to option a book, got very excited
about it and it turns out the book was entangled

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:36.200
in another country even though the rights
had run out, the other country had a tax credit

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:42.600
for the film and gave them advance funds which
I didn’t know this but it legally terminates

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.090
any kind of future purchase because the money
is already at play, I never knew that.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:51.350
Film Courage: It’s fascinating information
because I would never think that either.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:52.370
These are all things that…

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:55.050
Jay: I don’t think the writer of the book
knew that either.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.230
Film Courage: Wow.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.650
So once you found out that this is probably
a no-go…

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.020
Jay: This was just a week ago…

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.450
Film Courage: It was a week ago?

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.650
Okay…how do you kind of get back on the
horse and start looking for something?

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:08.710
Jay: I started immediately.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:12.550
I mean a filmmaker doesn’t typically have
just one project that he’s interested in

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:13.580
doing.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.470
In my case I have a pretty important mandate.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:25.230
I’m only looking to do projects that are
personally beneficial to the world.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.840
And I’m not talking about pollution or something
like that.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:38.130
I’m talking about a meaningful project that
would help people engage and see things.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.550
Filmmaking is so brilliant.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:46.070
I just saw a great movie I can mention it
called THE INSULT and it’s a masterpiece!

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.040
Why is it a masterpiece?

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:53.800
Go see the movie and you’ll understand because
it talks about…it didn’t matter that it

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.670
was filmed in Lebanon.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:59.350
What mattered to me was it could have been
anywhere.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:04.560
And whether you are talking positive about
politics or negative, it could have happened

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:05.670
anywhere.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.270
And it was not a true story, it was fiction.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:15.510
But the way it was filmed and the way it was
written really tells a great story about tolerance

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.660
and talks about hatred.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:24.810
And when you see a film like that and you
walk out, you are affected in a way almost

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.459
Shakespearean.

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:30.670
And that’s the kind of films that I want
to make.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:37.480
Films that viscerally help you understand
the world more and maybe make you more tolerant,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:46.330
maybe make you more perceptive and certainly
filmmaking is one of the best vehicles other

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.250
than speech or a book.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:57.090
And to me a filmmaker really has an opportunity
to tell a story with the belief (not in a

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.790
purposeful preaching) but the belief is you’ll
walk out and you’ll have a perspective you

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:02.750
might never have had.

